    fields:  # c9dc
      statement: "A move in any self-claim verdict, caveat set, or credited-flow bit must be acknowledged by a human re-recording the snapshot and correcting SECURITY.md; the gate detected this move at the first firing after the introducing commit, and nothing delivered it."  # c9dc
      root_cause: "a33de088bc (INV-foluz, Phase A): every bare builtin call now emits an edge naming `builtins` in the module slot, and python.yaml's module_completeness has no `builtins` entry, so _uncatalogued_external_modules returns it and _call_production_coverage sets qualifying_only=False on every claim -- the verify_claims.py:3898 inconclusive-without-caveats arm. Reproduced locally: the gate's own command yields the identical 40-line drift. The fix is a catalogue completeness declaration for builtins resting on new rows (print/breakpoint/copyright/credits -> logging; license -> fs_read+logging; help -> logging+subprocess), not a gate change."  # c9dc
- op: discuss  # fb2d
  at: '2026-09-03T04:11:00Z'  # fb2d
  by: agent  # fb2d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fb2d
  clock: 3  # fb2d
  nonce: fb2d  # fb2d
  message: "ROOT CAUSE ATTRIBUTED (pending the local verifier's own `details` string as\nthe last confirmation).\n\nTHE MECHANISM, READ OUT OF THE GATE. `inconclusive` with EMPTY caveats is the\nverify_claims.py:3898 arm: `_call_production_coverage` returned\n`complete=False` with `qualifying_only=False`. Of its branches, the one that\nmatches every observed fact is `_uncatalogued_external_modules`: a dst that\nNAMES a module the catalogue has not ENUMERATED (`module_io_is_enumerated`,\nexact-match against python.yaml's `module_completeness`) is \"none I could\nsee\", and one such module withholds every clean verdict.\n\nTHE INTRODUCING COMMIT IS a33de088bc (INV-foluz, Phase A). Before it, py.py\nemitted a call edge for a bare builtin only when the name was `open`, so\n`builtins` reached the gate only at sites that already carried a row\n(classify_call hit -> skipped). After it, EVERY bare builtin call emits\n`python:builtins:0-0:<name>:...` -- len, str, isinstance, getattr -- and\n`builtins` has three rows (open x2, input) and NO completeness entry.\nConfirmed on the shipped predicates: module_hint_disjuncts(\"builtins\") ==\n[[\"builtins\"]], is_definitionally_first_party(\"python\",\"builtins\") == False,\nand \"builtins\" is absent from module_completeness (the grep hits at\npython.yaml:187/249/499 are primitive rows, not the audit list).\n\nThe other window commits are excluded: 28998e145c is haskell and the tree\nholds zero .hs files; b730e10d41 touches go.yaml and a per-row abstention\nfield the gate does not read; 5a7aa6ce6a unions two walk-marker fields and\nsays \"no verdict moves\"; c05d573e69 is docs.\n\nWHY THE PER-PR GATE DID NOT CATCH IT. The self-claims gate left the per-PR\npipeline on 2026-08-30 (owner decision; ~14 min critical path). Detection is\ncron-bounded, and the cron's verdict was one bit.\n\nTHE FIX IS A CATALOGUE COMPLETENESS DECLARATION, NOT A GATE CHANGE. The gate\nis right: an unenumerated module IS unexamined. `builtins` must be enumerated\nunder the survey's own rule (every I/O surface rowed or absent). Walked by\nhand over dir(builtins) on 3.12, because the probe cannot see a C module:\nprint / breakpoint / copyright / credits (logging), license (fs_read +\nlogging, simultaneous), help (logging + subprocess pager, unruled) gain rows;\nopen and input already had them; exit/quit raise SystemExit; exec/eval/compile\nare in-process; __import__ is a dunder outside the audit's public-callable\nrule and is named as such rather than left implicit. Then a dated\ncompleteness entry, a survey addendum, tests against the SHIPPED catalogue\n(a stand-in could not have caught this), and the gate's own command re-run\nlocally: the expected outcome is every claim returning to the recorded\nsnapshot with NO snapshot update."  # fb2d
